                <pb facs="0311" n="193" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg193"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.00/2620a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the High Commissioner in
                        Haiti</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Russell</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-12-06T11:00:00-05:00">December 6, 1929—11
                        a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>63. While temporary detention of persons fomenting disorder may of course prove
                    necessary the Department would not approve the trial of Haitians for serious
                    offenses by military courts or the imposition of heavy sentences except in
                    extreme cases and after the Department had had an opportunity to consider the
                    facts and approve in advance the institution of proceedings.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
